The central rule is: **profile the script’s workload, not the general-purpose interpreter**.

A named Python workload profile installed as `/etc/apparmor.d/organization.acme-python-job`:

```apparmor
#include <tunables/global>

@{ACME_ROOT}=/opt/acme
@{ACME_STATE}=/var/lib/acme
@{ACME_CACHE}=/var/cache/acme

profile organization.acme-python-job {
    #include <abstractions/base>
    #include if exists <abstractions/python>
    #include if exists <abstractions/nameservice-strict>

    # Interpreter and immutable application code.
    /usr/bin/python3{,.*} mr,
    @{ACME_ROOT}/ r,
    @{ACME_ROOT}/** r,

    # Explicit virtual environment, if used.
    @{ACME_ROOT}/venv/ r,
    @{ACME_ROOT}/venv/** mr,

    # State and scratch space.
    @{ACME_STATE}/ r,
    @{ACME_STATE}/** rwk,
    @{ACME_CACHE}/ r,
    @{ACME_CACHE}/** rwk,
    owner /tmp/acme-python-*/ rw,
    owner /tmp/acme-python-*/** rwk,

    # Network only if this workload is a client.
    network inet stream,
    network inet6 stream,

    # Explicit helper programs. Avoid generic /** ix.
    /usr/bin/logger ix,
    /usr/bin/openssl ix,

    # Guard common secrets even if a future abstraction becomes broader.
    audit deny owner @{HOME}/.ssh/** rwklx,
    audit deny owner @{HOME}/.gnupg/** rwklx,
    audit deny owner @{HOME}/.config/systemd/** wklx,

    # No privilege escalation helpers.
    audit deny /usr/bin/{sudo,su,pkexec} x,
    audit deny /bin/su x,

    # Site-specific additions.
    #include if exists <local/organization.acme-python-job>
}
```

Launch it consistently:

```bash
/usr/bin/aa-exec -p organization.acme-python-job -- \
    /usr/bin/python3 -I /opt/acme/bin/job.py
```

Python’s `-I` isolated mode is useful defense in depth because it reduces environment and user-site influence, but AppArmor must still restrict the actual import paths and writable application directories. The interpreter binary may be root-owned while imported modules, startup files, plugins, package caches, or current-directory content are attacker-controlled.

A Perl profile follows the same pattern:

```apparmor
