`aa_change_onexec()` defers the transition until the next execution and overrides an ordinary execution transition. File descriptors already open before a transition can remain usable, so wrappers and parent processes must deliberately close or mark unwanted descriptors close-on-exec. This is a critical reason not to assume that entering a stricter profile automatically revokes inherited access. citeturn14search0turn14search7turn14search22

### Browser profile

A browser combines several layers: a privileged browser process, sandboxed renderers, GPU processes, media processes, extension processes, crash handlers, and sometimes updater or password-store integrations. Chromium uses namespaces and seccomp among its Linux sandbox mechanisms; Firefox also uses a multiprocess Linux sandbox and syscall brokering. AppArmor should supplement these mechanisms, never replace them. In particular, do not use `--no-sandbox` to resolve AppArmor denials. Chromium’s own documentation describes that option as disabling critical security features. citeturn16search11turn16search3turn5search2

A browser template:

```apparmor
#include <tunables/global>

profile organization.browser-wayland {
    #include <abstractions/base>
    #include if exists <abstractions/nameservice>
    #include if exists <abstractions/ssl_certs>
    #include if exists <abstractions/fonts>
    #include if exists <abstractions/audio>
    #include if exists <abstractions/organization/wayland-client>
    #include if exists <abstractions/organization/gpu-intel-render>
    #include if exists <abstractions/organization/gpu-nvidia-render>

    # Browser-internal namespace sandbox. Remove only if the tested browser
    # genuinely does not require it.
    userns,

    # Firefox ESR examples; adjust to the installed package.
    /usr/bin/firefox-esr rix,
    /usr/lib/firefox-esr/ r,
    /usr/lib/firefox-esr/** mr,

    # Chromium alternatives.
    # /usr/bin/chromium rix,
    # /usr/lib/chromium/ r,
    # /usr/lib/chromium/** mr,

    # Browser-owned state.
    owner @{HOME}/.mozilla/ r,
    owner @{HOME}/.mozilla/** rwk,
    owner @{HOME}/.cache/mozilla/ r,
    owner @{HOME}/.cache/mozilla/** rwk,

    # For Chromium, replace or supplement:
    # owner @{HOME}/.config/chromium/** rwk,
    # owner @{HOME}/.cache/chromium/** rwk,

    # Deliberately selected user-visible directories.
    owner @{XDG_DOWNLOAD_DIR}/ r,
    owner @{XDG_DOWNLOAD_DIR}/** rwk,
    owner @{XDG_DOCUMENTS_DIR}/ r,
    owner @{XDG_DOCUMENTS_DIR}/** r,

    # Internet access and local resolver traffic.
    network inet stream,
    network inet6 stream,
    network inet dgram,
    network inet6 dgram,

    # Common desktop integrations. Narrow after log-driven discovery.
    #include if exists <abstractions/dbus-session>

    # Sensitive material outside browser scope.
    audit deny owner @{HOME}/.ssh/** rwklx,
    audit deny owner @{HOME}/.gnupg/** rwklx,
    audit deny owner @{HOME}/.aws/** rwklx,
    audit deny owner @{HOME}/.kube/** rwklx,
    audit deny owner @{HOME}/.config/systemd/** wklx,
    audit deny owner @{HOME}/.local/share/keyrings/** r,

    audit deny /usr/bin/{sudo,su,pkexec} x,

    # XWayland compatibility, only if required.
    #include if exists <abstractions/X>

    #include if exists <local/organization.browser-wayland>
}
```

For production, use separate profiles for Firefox and Chromium because their package paths, process launches, state directories, sandbox requirements, crash reporters, and desktop integrations differ. Prefer a dedicated browser profile per user-data directory when distinct identities are valuable—for example, corporate browsing, personal browsing, and untrusted research.

The `userns,` rule should be treated as an explicit attack-surface decision. Chromium’s namespace sandbox uses unprivileged user namespaces, while the kernel warns that user namespaces expose resource-management risks and recommends memory cgroups on systems where users or their programs are not trusted. Preserve browser namespaces, pair them with cgroup resource limits, and use AppArmor to limit the surrounding filesystem and device view. citeturn16search7turn16search34

