1264:Docker normally applies a generated `docker-default` AppArmor profile to container payloads and allows custom profiles to be selected. Podman supports profile selection through its security options. Do not use `apparmor=unconfined` or privileged-container mode as a routine GPU compatibility fix: privileged containers weaken AppArmor, seccomp, namespace, and device controls. citeturn6search0turn6search3turn6search1turn6search22
1265-
1266-Use three distinct boundaries:
1267-
1268-| Boundary | Profile subject | Typical policy |
1269-|---|---|---|
1270:| Runtime control plane | `dockerd`, `containerd`, Podman helper processes | Runtime state, sockets, cgroups, mounts, image storage, networking, namespace creation |
1271-| Container payload | Process inside the container | Application files, network, capabilities, mounts, devices |
1272-| Host launcher or orchestrator | Service invoking the runtime | Runtime socket and selected command access, not direct payload privileges |
1273-
1274-For GPU containers, coordinate:
1275-
1276-```text
1277-Host device existence and DAC groups
1278-Container runtime device passthrough
1279:systemd DeviceAllow where applicable
1280-Container cgroup device/BPF rules
1281:Host or container AppArmor profile
1282-NVIDIA container hooks and mounted libraries
1283-User namespace or rootless-runtime mapping
1284-```
1285-
1286:Do not attach an application profile intended for a normal executable directly to the container runtime. The runtime needs namespace, mount, cgroup, and filesystem operations that the payload should never receive. Profile stacking and AppArmor policy namespaces are advanced mechanisms and should be introduced only after ordinary host and payload profiles are demonstrably correct. citeturn11search13turn6search24
