1550-### Common troubleshooting patterns
1551-
1552-| Symptom | Likely cause | Investigation and remedy |
1553-|---|---|---|
1554:| Profile is loaded but application is unconfined | Existing process predates profile; attachment path does not match real ELF; application bypassed wrapper | Resolve `/proc/<pid>/exe`, inspect `/proc/<pid>/attr/current`, restart through intended launcher |
1555:| Script remains unconfined | Profile attached to interpreter incorrectly, shebang path differs, or direct execution bypasses named profile | Use `aa-exec` or immutable ELF wrapper; verify resolved interpreter |
1556:| Browser fails immediately | Missing namespace, shared-library, runtime-dir, or sandbox-helper permission | Check audit logs and browser sandbox diagnostics; do not add `--no-sandbox` |
1557-| Browser works only with broad home access | File chooser, password store, extension, portal, or download rules conflated | Separate browser state, approved user directories, portal D-Bus rules, and secrets integration |
1558-| GUI opens but cannot display | Missing Wayland socket or runtime directory access | Include and inspect the Wayland abstraction; verify `WAYLAND_DISPLAY` and runtime path |
1559-| GUI displays but software-renders | Missing render node, driver library, shader cache, or sysfs enumeration | Check `/dev/dri/renderD*`, Mesa/NVIDIA library paths, and GPU diagnostics |
1560:| Labwc starts but launched apps fail | Children inherit compositor profile | Create explicit transitions or route launches through wrappers/user services |
1561-| XWayland app fails while Wayland app works | Missing X abstraction, X socket, or Xauthority access | Add XWayland-specific rules only to affected profile |
1562:| CUDA reports no device | Missing `nvidiactl`, GPU node, UVM node, library, DAC group, or systemd DeviceAllow | Check node ownership, AppArmor logs, unit device policy, `nvidia-smi`, and UVM module |
1563-| CUDA works unconfined but not as service | `PrivateDevices=yes`, different service user/groups, or missing unit environment | Disable private device namespace, use selected `DeviceAllow`, set groups and environment explicitly |
1564:| D-Bus operation fails with no file denial | D-Bus mediation or peer restrictions | Search AppArmor D-Bus audit records; identify bus, interface, member, path, and peer |
1565:| No denial appears | Audit rate limiting, wrong log source, operation blocked by DAC/systemd/seccomp first | Check `journalctl -k`, auditd, service logs, DAC modes, cgroup devices, and seccomp |
1566-| Profile reload appears ineffective | Process was unconfined, wrong profile name, or cache/source mismatch | Inspect label and parser output; reload with cache bypass when diagnosing; restart |
1567:| `aa-logprof` proposes broad rules | Test exercised too much behavior or abstraction suggestions are broad | Reject suggestion, reproduce one operation, write narrower rule manually |
1568:| Container starts only as privileged | Device, mount, namespace, seccomp, or AppArmor policy is missing | Identify exact missing control; do not retain privileged mode as the fix |
1569:| User service rejects `AppArmorProfile=` | Directive unsupported in user service manager | Use attached executable or `aa-exec` in `ExecStart` |
1570:| Policy breaks after package upgrade | Executable, library, helper, or state path changed | Include package-upgrade tests and compare process execution graph |
1571-| Unexpected access survives transition | File descriptor was opened before transition | Close or mark descriptors close-on-exec in launcher; inspect parent process |
1572-
1573:Profile replacement affects already-confined processes, but open file descriptors may survive policy changes and profile transitions. AppArmor also mediates by the kernel-resolved path, so symlinks, aliases, mount namespaces, deleted files, and container paths deserve explicit testing. The `attach_disconnected` flag is documented as unsafe and intended for debugging or policy development, not as a general solution to namespace-related denials. citeturn11search16turn14search7turn18search0
1574-
1575-### User-namespace pitfalls
1576-
1577-Do not treat `userns,` as either universally safe or universally forbidden.
1578-
1579:Browsers and rootless container engines may depend on user namespaces for their own privilege separation. Denying them can force applications toward weaker fallbacks or complete failure. Conversely, allowing namespace creation exposes additional kernel code and resource-accounting concerns. The correct pattern is workload-specific allowance, memory and process cgroup limits, seccomp retention, current kernels, and removal of unnecessary namespace types through systemd where that does not conflict with the application’s sandbox. citeturn16search3turn16search7turn16search34
1580-
1581-For a service that needs no namespaces:
1582-
1583-```ini
1584-RestrictNamespaces=yes
1585-```
1586-
1587:For one that needs only a reviewed subset, use explicit systemd namespace settings supported by the installed systemd version. Do not combine a blanket `RestrictNamespaces=yes` with a browser and then “fix” the browser by disabling its internal sandbox.
