1589-## Maintenance, CI, and security trade-offs
1590-
1591-### Policy lifecycle
1592-
1593:Treat AppArmor policy as production code:
1594-
1595-```text
1596-Threat model
1597-    ↓
--
1613-    ↓
1614-Application, kernel, driver, and policy updates
1615-```
1616-
1617:Policy changes should require the same level of review as privilege changes in application code. A one-line abstraction modification may expand dozens of profiles; a new `ix` rule can permit an unexpected helper to execute under a powerful parent domain; a new writable module path may let a confined identity redefine code; and a new GPU node can expose an additional driver interface.
1618-
1619-### CI pipeline
1620-
1621-A practical pipeline contains:
1622-
1623-| Stage | Checks |
1624-|---|---|
1625:| Formatting | Stable ordering, comments, naming convention, final local hook |
1626:| Parser | `apparmor_parser -Q`, warnings, deprecated syntax, feature ABI compatibility |
1627-| Lint | Ban or review `ux`, fallback-to-unconfined transitions, broad globs, blanket capabilities, blanket network, `/dev/**`, writable code |
1628:| Package | Correct file ownership, permissions, install paths, conffile behavior, post-install reload |
1629:| VM integration | Boot with AppArmor active, load profiles, start services, verify labels |
1630-| Positive tests | Application-specific functional matrix |
1631-| Negative tests | Secret, filesystem, network, execution, namespace, and device denials |
1632:| systemd | `systemd-analyze verify` and security review |
1633-| Desktop | Labwc session, Wayland socket, portals, audio, render acceleration |
1634-| GPU | Intel/Mesa or NVIDIA enumeration, rendering, CUDA |
1635-| Container | Custom payload profile, rootless/rootful mode, GPU passthrough where supported |
1636-| Upgrade | Old-policy/new-app and new-policy/old-app compatibility where rollout sequencing requires it |
1637:| Performance | Startup, process spawning, browser tabs, shader cache, CUDA job launch |
1638-| Artifact | Signed package, manifest, checksums, release notes, rollback profile |
1639-
1640:`systemd-analyze verify` can statically validate unit files, while `apparmor_parser -Q` can compile policy without changing the running kernel. Full enforcement tests still require a VM or bare-metal runner with the AppArmor LSM active; an ordinary unprivileged CI container cannot prove kernel enforcement, systemd device policy, Wayland behavior, or GPU compatibility. citeturn6search20turn19view1
